---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import mrskTopology from '../../assets/research/mrsk/fig1-system-topology.png';
import mrskRatioPdfs from '../../assets/research/mrsk/fig5-ratio-pdfs.png';
import mrskMolecules from '../../assets/research/mrsk/fig3-molecules-per-bit.png';
import mrskAdaptive from '../../assets/research/mrsk/fig4-adaptive-mapping.png';
import mrskBerTb from '../../assets/research/mrsk/fig12-ber-vs-symbol-time.png';
import mrskBerQ from '../../assets/research/mrsk/fig13-ber-vs-molecules.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="MRSK Modulation"
  description="Multi Ratio Shift Keying: a molecular communication modulation scheme that encodes information in the ratios of several molecule types, giving a detection threshold independent of the channel."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2025 · Koç University, Center for neXt-generation Communications</p>
      <h1>Multi Ratio Shift Keying (MRSK) Modulation for Molecular Communication</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        Molecular communication sends information by releasing molecules into a fluid and
        letting them diffuse. Diffusion is slow, so molecules from earlier symbols are still
        arriving when the next one is sent — the dominant error source in the field. MRSK
        sidesteps it by encoding into the <em>ratios</em> of several molecule types rather than
        their absolute counts, which makes the detection threshold independent of the channel.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>First author · with Prof. Özgür B. Akan</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">molecular communication</span>
            <span class="tag">modulation</span>
            <span class="tag">ratio distributions</span>
            <span class="tag">bit error rate</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Published</dt>
          <dd class="links">
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TCOMM.2025.3581006" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEEE Trans. Communications 73(10), 2025 ↗</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The idea</h2>
      <p>
        Existing molecular modulation schemes encode into one of three properties: concentration
        (OOK, CSK, PAM), molecule type (MoSK), or release time (RTSK). All of them share a
        weakness — the quantity carrying the information decays with distance and time, so the
        receiver's decision threshold has to track the channel. Get the channel estimate wrong and
        the symbol is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        MRSK encodes into the ratio of the concentrations of different molecule types, and
        specifically into <em>all</em> the ratios available within a set of them. The transmitter
        holds <em>N</em> molecule species in separate reservoirs; a modulator selects them into a
        mixing chamber and releases them together as a concentration spike. The receiver counts what
        arrives and reads the ratio.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mrskTopology} alt="MRSK system topology: N molecule reservoirs feed a modulator and mixing chamber, molecules diffuse across the channel over distance d to a spherical receiver of radius r, then an output transducer and demodulator recover the bits." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          General systems topology of MRSK. <em>N</em> molecule reservoirs feed a modulator and
          mixing chamber; molecules diffuse across the channel over distance <em>d</em> to a
          receiver of radius <em>r</em>, where an output transducer and demodulator recover the
          bits.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Why a ratio is the right thing to send</h2>
      <p>
        The argument turns on one observation. If the molecule species share the same diffusion
        coefficient, their spatiotemporal distributions track each other as they spread. The
        absolute count at the receiver falls off with distance and time; the <em>expected ratio</em>
        does not — it is constant in space and time.
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>η</em> = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>X</em></span><span class="den"><em>Y</em></span></span>
         →  ⟨<em>η</em>⟩ independent of <em>d</em> and <em>t</em>
      </div>

      <p>That single fact buys several things at once:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A fixed detection threshold.</strong> Because the expected ratio does not move,
          the threshold does not have to be re-estimated per link or per channel condition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Suitability for one-to-many links.</strong> A transmitter can serve several
          receivers at once without retuning modulation or demodulation parameters for each,
          making non-coherent detection across a SIMO system practical.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lighter distribution tails.</strong> The probability density of a ratio of
          Gaussians has lighter tails than the individual Gaussians built from the same molecule
          count, which is where the resilience to inter-symbol interference and Gaussian noise
          comes from.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Freedom in the molecule budget.</strong> The same ratio can be produced with any
          total number of molecules — useful when resources are scarce.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The idea has precedent in biology, which is part of what makes it appealing for systems
        meant to operate inside a body. Rats can distinguish binary odour mixtures by the molar
        ratio of their components, recognising the same mixture across varying absolute
        concentrations. In metabolic networks, relative concentrations — the ATP/ADP balance in
        glycolysis, for instance — regulate enzyme activity and pathway flux rather than absolute
        quantities.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ratio-based encoding had been tried before, in Isomer Ratio Shift Keying and later in Ratio
        Shift Keying, but both use only the ratio of <em>two</em> molecule types and both assume
        symbol intervals long enough that channel memory can be ignored. MRSK drops both
        restrictions: an M-ary scheme over all ratios of an arbitrary molecule set, with memory
        accounted for.
      </p>

      <CriticalityDivider label="the mathematics" />

      <h2>Making the ratio tractable</h2>
      <p>
        Encoding into a ratio means the detection statistic is a ratio of random variables, and the
        statistics of Gaussian ratio distributions in this setting were largely unexplored. Deriving
        the bit error rate precisely required building that framework — the distribution of ratios
        of noncentral Gaussian random variables — and then finding an approximation tractable enough
        to use.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mrskRatioPdfs} alt="Three probability density plots of the received ratio, centred near 1, e, and e to the minus one, each overlaying sampled, exact, solid-approximation, Gaussian-approximation and simulated curves." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          PDFs of the received ratio when the transmitted ratios are 1, <em>e</em>, and
          <em>e</em><sup>−1</sup>. The exact expression, the solid approximation and the Gaussian
          approximation agree closely; the particle-based simulation sits slightly lower at the peak
          and heavier in the tails.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        That agreement is what justifies using the tractable approximation for the BER derivation
        instead of the exact ratio distribution. Simulations used a transmitter–receiver distance
        <em>d</em> = 10 µm, receiver radius <em>r</em> = 5 µm, diffusion coefficient
        <em>D</em> = 79.4 µm²/s, and a channel memory of <em>L</em> = 5 symbols.
      </p>

      <CriticalityDivider label="the cost" />

      <h2>The molecule budget, and what to do about it</h2>
      <p>
        MRSK is not free. Encoding into ratios consumes molecules heavily, and nanomachines operate
        exactly where resources are scarce — the reservoirs will eventually run dry.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mrskMolecules} alt="Surface plot of average moles used per bit against the number of molecule types N and the modulation order M." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Average number of molecules used per bit across a range of <em>N</em>, <em>M</em>
          parameters — the honest cost curve of the scheme.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The answer is to stop treating the symbol-to-molecule assignment as fixed. The transmitter
        watches how much of each species remains and reassigns the mapping accordingly: the most
        frequently used symbol goes to the most abundant species, the least used symbol to the
        scarcest, and the rest are distributed by redundancy, with the ordering refreshed whenever
        it changes.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mrskAdaptive} alt="Adaptive versus non-adaptive transmission: average steps until reservoir depletion against the number of molecule types N, with percentage improvement rising from 39.1 percent to 86.9 percent." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Maximum number of messages transmittable with and without the adaptive scheme, for varying
          <em>N</em>. Adaptive mapping extends transmission longevity by 39.1 % at <em>N</em> = 2,
          rising to 86.9 % at <em>N</em> = 10 — the benefit grows with the number of molecule types.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What came out</h2>
      <p>
        Against every conventional scheme compared — OOK, CSK, PAM, MoSK, RTSK, and ADMC — MRSK
        gives better bit error rate. The two comparisons below are the ones that matter in practice,
        because they sweep the two resources a designer actually trades against each other: how long
        each symbol is allowed to take, and how many molecules it is allowed to spend.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mrskBerTb} alt="Bit error rate against bit time for BCSK, OOK, MRSK, RTSK and MoSK on logarithmic axes; MRSK falls steeply to below ten to the minus ten while the others remain far higher." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          BER against bit time <em>t</em><sub>b</sub> for the compared schemes. MRSK separates from
          the others by many orders of magnitude, and the gap <em>widens</em> as <em>t</em><sub>b</sub>
          grows — which identifies inter-symbol interference, not noise, as the factor limiting the
          scheme.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The second sweep is over <em>Q</em>, the base number of molecules per release. Raising
        <em>Q</em> narrows the received distributions relative to their means and pushes them further
        apart, so they overlap less and misdetection becomes less likely; MRSK's BER falls roughly
        exponentially in <em>Q</em>. The contrast with RTSK is instructive rather than incidental —
        its BER is flat across the whole range, because detection based on the first arriving
        molecule does not care how many were sent behind it.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={mrskBerQ} alt="Bit error rate against base molecule number Q for BCSK, OOK, MRSK, RTSK and MoSK; MRSK decreases steadily with Q while the other schemes stay nearly flat." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          BER against the base molecule number <em>Q</em>. MRSK improves steadily with the molecule
          budget; the schemes it is compared against are close to flat over the same range.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Two design results are worth stating plainly, because they cut against the instinct that more
        is better. Under moderate channel conditions, binary modulation with the <em>fewest</em>
        molecule types is optimal. It is only when bit times must be short that higher-order
        modulation, or more molecule types, earns its keep by mitigating inter-symbol interference —
        which is what makes the scheme suited to high data rates.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also a convex relationship between the ratio range and the BER, so the spacing of
        the ratios is a real parameter to optimise rather than a free choice.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest limits</h2>
      <p>
        The molecule cost is the main one: MRSK can require more molecules than conventional
        schemes, so it belongs in applications that demand high precision in environments where
        molecules are abundant, not in the most starved regimes. The channel-invariance argument
        also rests on the molecule species sharing a diffusion coefficient; how far that can be
        relaxed is not something this work settles. The authors name three directions from here:
        refining threshold optimisation under varying channel conditions, testing resilience in more
        diverse molecular networks, and integrating the scheme into real biological and
        nanotechnology-based platforms.
      </p>
      <p class="credit">
        Figures © 2025 IEEE, reproduced from the article above by the author.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        A separate strand of this work, with the same group, asks what happens when the nanomachines
        stop being point-to-point transceivers and start behaving as a collective — see
        <a href={base + '/projects/neural-inspired-molecular-communication'}>neural-inspired molecular communication networks</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="proj-foot">
      <a href={base + '/research'}>← Back to research</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .shell { max-width: 44rem; }
  .back { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .back a, .proj-foot a { color: var(--muted); }
  .back a:hover, .proj-foot a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  .proj-head { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .proj-head h1 { margin: 0.15em 0 0.35em; font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1.12; }
  .proj-blurb { font-size: 1.18rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 40rem; }
  .proj-meta {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0; display: grid; gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .proj-meta > div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; align-items: baseline; }
  .proj-meta dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
  }
  .proj-meta dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 0.5rem; }
  .tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.74rem;
    color: var(--accent-deep); background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem; border-radius: 3px;
  }
  .links { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.3rem; align-items: start; }
  .links a { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.86rem; }

  .fig { margin: 2rem 0; }
  .fig :global(img) {
    display: block; width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--faint); border-radius: 4px; background: #fff;
  }
  .fig figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.6rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .eq {
    margin: 1.4rem 0; padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    text-align: center; font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.9;
    background: var(--paper-raised);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .eq em { font-style: italic; }
  .frac { display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; margin: 0 0.15rem; }
  .frac .num { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 0 0.35rem 0.1rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .frac .den { padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem 0; line-height: 1.35; }

  .credit {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.74rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .aside {
    margin-top: 2rem; padding-top: 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
    font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--muted);
  }

  .proj-foot {
    margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .proj-meta > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
